# =========================================================================
# The Poly Mailers — environment variables
#
# Copy this file to `.env` for local development, and set the same variables
# in your hosting provider's environment settings for production.
#
# `.env` is git-ignored. Never commit real credentials, and never place them
# in a settings file, a permission rule or any client-side code — every value
# below is read server-side only, inside src/pages/api/quote.ts.
# =========================================================================

# --- SMTP: required for the quote and contact forms to send -------------
# Without these three, the endpoint returns a polite failure to the visitor
# and logs the misconfiguration server-side. It never exposes which value is
# missing to the browser.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=

# --- Where enquiries are delivered --------------------------------------
SMTP_TO=info@thepolymailers.com

# --- How enquiry emails are addressed -----------------------------------
# The From address must be one your SMTP provider is authorised to send as,
# or SPF and DKIM will fail and messages will land in spam. The enquirer's
# own address is set as Reply-To rather than as From, which is both correct
# for deliverability and the reason header injection is not possible here.
SMTP_FROM_NAME=The Poly Mailers
SMTP_FROM_EMAIL=info@thepolymailers.com

# =========================================================================
# Notes
#
# Port 465 is treated as implicit TLS; anything else uses STARTTLS. Set
# SMTP_PORT to match what your provider documents.
#
# Rate limiting on the quote endpoint is in-memory and per instance. It is a
# speed bump against scripted abuse rather than a distributed limiter. If a
# hard guarantee is needed, replace the store in src/pages/api/quote.ts with
# a shared one (Upstash, Vercel KV or similar) — the interface is deliberately
# small enough to swap without touching the validation logic.
#
# No analytics, advertising or third-party tag variables exist, because the
# site loads none of those. Adding one would also require updating the
# Content-Security-Policy in vercel.json, which currently permits no external
# origins at all.
# =========================================================================
